            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R2-001",
                "lens": "readability",
                "location": "engine/reviewverdict/decide.go:86-89",
                "severity": "SUGGESTION",
                "claim": "The unexported fate constants `blocks` and `unchecked` sit beside the exported `Blocks` and `Unverified` outcomes and differ from them only in case or by near-synonym. A reader can easily confuse the per-finding fate with the review-level outcome. `refuted` also has no case in Decide's switch and is dropped silently. Distinct names (for example fateBlocks, fateUnchecked) or a one-line note that refuted is intentionally unlisted would make this clearer. This is a maintainability suggestion, not a defect.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/reviewverdict/decide.go:11",
                  "engine/reviewverdict/decide.go:61-65",
                  "engine/reviewverdict/decide.go:86-89"
                ]
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Read both added files in full: decide.go (the rule and the fate helper) and decide_test.go (the table-driven tests).",
              "The Decide doc comment matches the severeFinding branches for unknown causality: a head NotReproduced is refuted, a base Reproduced is carried, a base NotReproduced falls through to the head decision, and a base NotRun is unverified. The test table in decide_test.go covers the same combinations.",
              "The empty `case NotReproduced:` in severeFinding relies on control leaving the inner switch and reaching the head switch. It carries an explanatory comment, so it is acceptable.",
              "The `default` severity branch treats unlisted values as severe, and the code comment says so. There are no unexplained constants and no dead or duplicated logic."
            ]
          }
        }
