            "lens": "review-reliability",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R3-001",
                "lens": "reliability",
                "location": "engine/reviewverdict/decide_test.go:157-181",
                "severity": "SUGGESTION",
                "claim": "The missing-verdict test only covers an introduced BLOCKER. Decide treats a missing verdict as the zero-value Verdict, so the same fail-safe path is taken for unknown-causality findings, but no test proves it there. If the zero value of Reproduction were ever changed to NotReproduced, an unknown-causality finding with no verdict could be silently refuted and the review could report Clear. Also not covered: duplicate verdict IDs (last one wins) and an unlisted severity (read as severe).",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/reviewverdict/decide.go:50-62",
                  "engine/reviewverdict/decide_test.go:168-181"
                ]
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Decide is a pure function, and the tests are table-driven over severity, disposition, head result and base result.",
              "I traced the unknown-causality branch of severeFinding against its doc comment and against the test table. All nine head/base combinations match, including the head-not-run, base-reproduced case that carries the finding.",
              "Blocking, Unverified, Fix and Carry are filled in the order of the result, and the outcome precedence is Blocks, then Unverified, then Clear. The ordering test checks both.",
              "The definition of Verdict and its zero value is not in this candidate, so the missing-verdict behaviour depends on code I cannot see. That is why the suggestion above is not stronger."
            ]
          }
        }
